WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.893
Transformer-based language models show a striking
ability called in-context learning,

00:00:05.973 --> 00:00:12.654
where a pretrained model solves brand-new tasks
just by seeing a few input-output examples in its prompt,

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:13.941
with no fine-tuning.

00:00:14.021 --> 00:00:18.320
But why training produces this ability, and how
well it generalizes,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.887
has stayed mostly a mystery because the math of
nonlinear attention and nonlinear activations is hard.

00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:30.360
This ICML 2024 paper gives the first theoretical
analysis of the training dynamics of

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.803
a Transformer with both nonlinear self-attention
and a nonlinear MLP,

00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:38.473
and proves when the trained model generalizes in-context,

00:00:38.553 --> 00:00:41.306
both in-domain and under distribution shift.

00:00:41.386 --> 00:00:46.264
It also delivers the first theory of how pruning
affects in-context learning,

00:00:46.344 --> 00:00:50.320
showing that removing the small-magnitude neurons
barely hurts.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:56.346
In-context learning lets a pretrained Transformer
handle new tasks by simply padding the

00:00:56.426 --> 00:00:59.664
query with a handful of example input-output pairs,

00:00:59.744 --> 00:01:01.225
no fine-tuning required.

00:01:01.305 --> 00:01:03.177
Despite its empirical success,

00:01:03.257 --> 00:01:08.317
the mechanics of how a Transformer is actually
trained to acquire this ability,

00:01:08.397 --> 00:01:11.766
and how far that ability generalizes, remain elusive.

00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:17.751
The core difficulty is technical: the
self-attention layer is nonlinear through the softmax,

00:01:17.831 --> 00:01:21.200
and the MLP is nonlinear through the ReLU activation,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.600
so the training problem is nonconvex and resists
the tools used for simpler models.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.118
Recent works began to explain in-context learning,

00:01:30.198 --> 00:01:32.693
but each keeps only part of the picture.

00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:35.331
Some ignore the nonlinear self-attention,

00:01:35.411 --> 00:01:38.614
others replace the nonlinear MLP with a linear one,

00:01:38.694 --> 00:01:42.475
and most study linear regression rather than
classification.

00:01:42.555 --> 00:01:50.070
None can characterize how to train a model that
generalizes under a distribution shift between training and test data,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.962
and none analyze how pruning a trained model
changes its in-context ability.

00:01:55.042 --> 00:02:00.497
Since practitioners routinely prune large language
models to save compute while hoping

00:02:00.577 --> 00:02:02.556
to keep their in-context skills,

00:02:02.636 --> 00:02:05.710
closing this theoretical gap matters in practice.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.981
This work makes three contributions.

00:02:10.061 --> 00:02:13.952
First,

00:02:14.032 --> 00:02:17.923
it is the first theoretical characterization of how
to train a Transformer that keeps

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:21.894
both nonlinear self-attention and a nonlinear MLP,

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:25.865
and it proves the trained model generalizes in
context to unseen tasks,

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:29.835
quantifying the required amount of data, number of
iterations, and context length.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:33.806
Second, it expands our understanding of the
mechanism of in-context learning,

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:37.777
showing how the attention layer and the MLP layer
cooperate to make correct predictions.

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:41.748
Third,

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:45.719
it provides the first theoretical analysis of
magnitude-based pruning for in-context learning,

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:49.690
proving that removing low-magnitude neurons is
essentially harmless.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:54.201
The model is deliberately minimal but nonlinear:
one self-attention head with a softmax,

00:02:54.281 --> 00:02:58.412
followed by a two-layer perceptron with ReLU activation.

00:02:58.492 --> 00:03:02.622
Training augments each query with a length-l
context of example pairs,

00:03:02.702 --> 00:03:06.833
embeds them into a prompt,

00:03:06.913 --> 00:03:11.044
and minimizes a hinge loss over prompts sampled
from a small subset of binary classification tasks.

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:15.255
Every input contains relevant patterns that
determine its label and irrelevant patterns that do not.

00:03:15.335 --> 00:03:19.465
The authors follow the gradient-descent dynamics of
the query, key, value,

00:03:19.545 --> 00:03:23.676
and MLP weights,

00:03:23.756 --> 00:03:27.887
and evaluate the resulting model both in-domain and
under a distribution shift where the

00:03:27.967 --> 00:03:32.098
test relevant patterns are linear combinations of
the training ones.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:36.308
Two quantities drive the guarantees: alpha,

00:03:36.388 --> 00:03:40.519
the fraction of context examples that share the
query's relevant pattern, and beta,

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.730
the magnitude of the relevant features.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:49.472
Experiments use a controlled synthetic setup that
matches the theory.

00:03:49.552 --> 00:03:54.235
There are six in-domain relevant patterns and
twenty-four irrelevant ones,

00:03:54.315 --> 00:03:58.290
and tasks are binary classifications built from
these patterns.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:03.118
Two models are trained: the one-layer Transformer
that the theory analyzes,

00:04:03.198 --> 00:04:07.045
and a small real-world GPT-2 with three layers and
two heads.

00:04:07.125 --> 00:04:12.903
Training uses a context length of twenty and a
relevant-pattern fraction of eighty percent,

00:04:12.983 --> 00:04:20.434
and out-of-domain evaluation uses new tasks whose
relevant patterns are linear combinations of the training patterns,

00:04:20.514 --> 00:04:22.880
exactly the regime the theorems cover.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:28.378
The main theorem shows that a number of iterations,
and a matching number of samples,

00:04:28.458 --> 00:04:33.636
that grow only polynomially in the problem
parameters is enough to drive the in-domain

00:04:33.716 --> 00:04:36.266
generalization error down to order epsilon.

00:04:36.346 --> 00:04:41.280
Crucially, the same trained model also generalizes
out of domain to shifted tasks,

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.477
as long as the new relevant patterns are linear
combinations of the training patterns

00:04:46.557 --> 00:04:49.046
with coefficients summing to at least one.

00:04:49.126 --> 00:04:54.427
The experiments confirm this sharply: the
out-of-domain classification error falls below

00:04:54.507 --> 00:04:58.585
one percent exactly when the combination strength
S-one reaches one,

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.358
and stays near zero above it,

00:05:00.438 --> 00:05:05.800
while the required context length and iterations
track the predicted dependence on alpha.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.326
Ablations vary the fraction of context examples
that share the query's relevant pattern.

00:05:10.406 --> 00:05:14.552
As predicted,

00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:18.778
the needed context length grows like one over alpha
and the needed iterations and samples

00:05:18.858 --> 00:05:23.004
grow like alpha to the minus two-thirds,

00:05:23.084 --> 00:05:27.230
so richer contexts converge faster and need shorter
prompts.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.456
A second comparison pits in-context learning
against logistic regression,

00:05:31.536 --> 00:05:35.682
kernel and linear SVMs, and nearest-neighbor classifiers.

00:05:35.762 --> 00:05:39.908
When the relevant fraction is high the gap is small,

00:05:39.988 --> 00:05:44.134
but in the harder low-fraction regime in-context
learning is the most sample-efficient method,

00:05:44.214 --> 00:05:48.360
indicating it removes irrelevant data and tolerates
label noise better than the classical baselines.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.337
A few numbers capture the paper's message.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.715
The training cost, both in iterations and in samples,

00:05:54.795 --> 00:05:57.265
scales as alpha to the minus two-thirds,

00:05:57.345 --> 00:06:00.451
and the required context length as one over alpha.

00:06:00.531 --> 00:06:04.212
The model needs to be trained on only a vanishing
fraction,

00:06:04.292 --> 00:06:09.375
order one over the square root of the number of
patterns, of all in-domain tasks,

00:06:09.455 --> 00:06:11.351
yet it generalizes to the rest.

00:06:11.431 --> 00:06:16.832
Out-of-domain error drops below one percent once
the combination strength reaches one.

00:06:16.912 --> 00:06:22.441
And on pruning: removing the low-magnitude neurons
keeps error at order epsilon plus one

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:24.353
over the square root of M-one,

00:06:24.433 --> 00:06:25.692
essentially lossless,

00:06:25.772 --> 00:06:31.619
and in experiments magnitude-based pruning of up to
about fifteen percent of the output-layer

00:06:31.699 --> 00:06:34.487
neurons leaves out-of-domain accuracy intact,

00:06:34.567 --> 00:06:40.670
whereas pruning the large-magnitude neurons
degrades error at least linearly in the pruning rate.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:46.540
The lasting message is that in-context learning in
a nonlinear Transformer is not a black

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:49.982
box: with both nonlinear attention and a nonlinear MLP,

00:06:50.062 --> 00:06:54.175
a one-layer model can be provably trained to
generalize in context,

00:06:54.255 --> 00:06:56.616
in-domain and under distribution shift,

00:06:56.696 --> 00:07:02.937
and the effort it takes is controlled by how much
of the context shares the query's relevant pattern.

00:07:03.017 --> 00:07:08.444
The trained model works by having attention focus
on the context examples that match the

00:07:08.524 --> 00:07:12.637
query's relevant pattern while the ReLU MLP
amplifies their labels.

00:07:12.717 --> 00:07:16.329
Because only the large-magnitude neurons carry this
signal,

00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:19.020
pruning the small ones is essentially free,

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:24.340
giving a principled reason why magnitude-based
pruning preserves in-context learning.
